\title{Branch-Set Minors, Chromatic Complexity, and the Wagner Bridge}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Hadwiger's conjecture predicts that every finite loopless graph $G$ contains a complete minor whose order is at least the chromatic number of $G$. We develop a self-contained branch-set framework for the conjecture. We define minor models and the Hadwiger number, prove reflexivity, the inclusion of spanning subgraphs as minors, monotonicity under adding edges, and the passage from cliques to complete minors. We establish the zero- and one-color indexed cases and prove that every finite $k$-degenerate graph is $(k+1)$-colorable by greedy elimination. At the five-vertex threshold, we identify the logical equivalence between the assertion that every non-four-colorable graph contains a $K_5$ minor and its contrapositive, that every $K_5$-minor-free graph is four-colorable. This yields the Four Color Theorem for graphs excluding both $K_5$ and $K_{3,3}$ as minors. We conclude with algorithms for small graphs, the density perspective, applications, and remaining structural questions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A proper coloring assigns colors to vertices so that adjacent vertices receive different colors. A graph minor, by contrast, is produced by deleting vertices or edges and contracting edges. These notions appear to measure different features: coloring records incompatibility, whereas contraction reveals coarse connectivity. Hadwiger's conjecture proposes a universal relation between them.

Let $K_t$ denote the complete graph on $t$ vertices and let $\chi(G)$ denote the chromatic number of a finite graph $G$. Hadwiger's conjecture states that if $\chi(G)\ge t$, then $G$ contains $K_t$ as a minor. Equivalently, if $h(G)$ is the largest $t$ for which $K_t$ is a minor of $G$, then
\[
\chi(G)\le h(G).
\]
The claim is stronger than the elementary observation that a clique of size $t$ requires $t$ colors. A graph may have chromatic number much larger than its largest visible clique; the conjecture predicts that a complete graph of matching order is nevertheless present after connected parts are compressed.

Contraction prevents a naive proof: it can increase chromatic number. Thus one cannot simply argue that every minor is easier to color. The conjecture instead says that high chromatic complexity forces a robust geometric obstruction---a family of disjoint connected regions that touch in every required pair.

This paper develops the branch-set foundation and the consequences that follow directly from it. The full conjecture is not asserted. The deep density result in Section~\ref{sec:density} is stated for context rather than derived.

\section{Graphs and colorings}

A graph means a simple undirected graph $G=(V,E)$. Its edges are unordered pairs of distinct vertices; loops and multiple edges are absent. We write $x\sim_G y$ when $x$ and $y$ are adjacent. Vertex sets are finite whenever coloring is discussed.

\begin{definition}[Proper coloring]
A proper $q$-coloring of $G$ is a function
\[
c:V\longrightarrow\{0,1,\ldots,q-1\}
\]
such that $c(x)\ne c(y)$ whenever $x\sim_G y$. The graph is $q$-colorable if such a function exists. Its chromatic number $\chi(G)$ is the least feasible $q$.
\end{definition}

The complete graph $K_t$ has $t$ vertices and every possible edge. A clique is a set of pairwise adjacent vertices, and $\omega(G)$ denotes the largest clique size. Since clique vertices need distinct colors,
\[
\omega(G)\le\chi(G).
\]
The complete bipartite graph $K_{r,s}$ has disjoint classes of sizes $r$ and $s$, all cross-class edges, and no within-class edges.

When $H$ and $G$ have the same vertex set, $H\subseteq G$ means that every edge of $H$ is an edge of $G$; in this case $H$ is a spanning subgraph.

\section{Branch-set minor models}

\begin{definition}[Minor model]
Let $G$ and $H$ be simple graphs. An $H$-minor model in $G$ assigns to each $u\in V(H)$ a branch set $B_u\subseteq V(G)$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $B_u$ is nonempty;
\item $B_u\cap B_v=\varnothing$ whenever $u\ne v$;
\item the subgraph of $G$ induced by $B_u$ is connected;
\item if $uv\in E(H)$, then some edge of $G$ has one endpoint in $B_u$ and the other in $B_v$.
\end{enumerate}
We say $H$ is a minor of $G$ if such a model exists.
\end{definition}

Contracting each connected branch set to one vertex and discarding unused material produces the target graph. Conversely, vertices merged by a sequence of contractions form branch sets. The model is a static, checkable certificate that avoids choosing an order of contractions.

\begin{definition}[Hadwiger number]
The Hadwiger number of $G$ is
\[
h(G)=\sup\{t\in\mathbb N:K_t\text{ is a minor of }G\}.
\]
For finite $G$, the supremum is a maximum because disjoint nonempty branch sets cannot outnumber the host vertices.
\end{definition}

\begin{conjecture}[Hadwiger]
Every finite simple graph satisfies $\chi(G)\le h(G)$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{definition}[Indexed Hadwiger coloring case]
For $k\ge0$, the $k$th indexed case asserts that every finite graph that is not $k$-colorable contains $K_{k+1}$ as a minor.
\end{definition}

If $\chi(G)=r$, the indexed assertion with $k=r-1$ yields a $K_r$ minor. Conversely, the Hadwiger inequality gives every indexed case.

\section{Structural properties}

\begin{lemma}[Singleton connectivity]
For every vertex $v$ of a graph $G$, the induced graph on $\{v\}$ is connected.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The sole vertex is connected to itself by a path of length zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reflexivity]
Every graph $G$ is a minor of itself.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each $v\in V(G)$ take $B_v=\{v\}$. These sets are nonempty, connected, and pairwise disjoint. Every target edge is the same edge in the host.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Spanning subgraphs are minors]
If $H\subseteq G$ on a common vertex set, then $H$ is a minor of $G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use singleton branch sets. Every edge required by $H$ is present in $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Empty spanning graph]
The graph on $V(G)$ with no edges is a minor of $G$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity under adding edges]
Suppose $H\subseteq G$ on the same vertex set. If $K$ is a minor of $H$, then $K$ is a minor of $G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Retain the branch sets of the model in $H$. Adding edges destroys neither disjointness nor nonemptiness. Every path in $H$ remains a path in $G$, and every edge realizing contact between branch sets remains present.
\end{proof}

This result concerns enlargement of a host on a fixed vertex set. Full transitivity requires composing branch-set families and proving connectivity of suitable unions; that stronger result is left for future work.

\begin{theorem}[Cliques yield complete minors]
If $G$ contains a clique of size $t$, then $K_t$ is a minor of $G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Index the clique vertices by $V(K_t)$ and use singleton branch sets. Every pair of branch sets is joined because the original set is a clique.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every graph $G$, $\omega(G)\le h(G)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
For every $t\ge0$, $h(K_t)\ge t$.
\end{corollary}

These statements isolate the easy direction. A literal complete subgraph is a complete minor; Hadwiger's conjecture becomes substantive when $\chi(G)>\omega(G)$.

\section{The first indexed cases}

\begin{proposition}
Every graph contains $K_0$ as a minor.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The target has no vertices, so all branch-set requirements are vacuous.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
Every graph with a nonempty vertex set contains $K_1$ as a minor.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Choose a host vertex and use its singleton as the sole branch set.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
If $G$ has an edge $uv$, then $K_2$ is a minor of $G$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The singletons $\{u\}$ and $\{v\}$ are disjoint connected branch sets, and $uv$ realizes the unique target edge.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-color indexed case]
Every finite graph that is not zero-colorable contains $K_1$ as a minor.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A graph is zero-colorable exactly when its vertex set is empty. Failure therefore supplies a vertex, and the preceding proposition applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-color indexed case]
Every finite graph that is not one-colorable contains $K_2$ as a minor.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An edgeless graph is one-colorable. Hence a graph that is not one-colorable contains an edge, whose endpoints form a $K_2$ model.
\end{proof}

The next case says that every non-bipartite graph contains a $K_3$ minor. Its standard route uses the odd-cycle characterization and contracts an odd cycle into three connected branch sets. That extension is not assumed here.

\section{Degeneracy and constructive coloring}

\begin{definition}[Degeneracy]
A finite graph $G$ is $k$-degenerate if every nonempty subset $S\subseteq V(G)$ contains a vertex with at most $k$ neighbors in $S$.
\end{definition}

Equivalently, every nonempty induced subgraph has minimum degree at most $k$. Repeatedly removing such a vertex produces an elimination ordering.

\begin{theorem}[Degeneracy coloring theorem]
Every finite $k$-degenerate graph is $(k+1)$-colorable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the number of vertices. The empty graph is immediate. In a nonempty graph choose a vertex $v$ of degree at most $k$. The induced graph $G-v$ remains $k$-degenerate, so by induction it has a proper coloring with $k+1$ colors. At most $k$ colors appear on neighbors of $v$, leaving at least one available color for $v$.
\end{proof}

The proof is algorithmic. Remove low-degree vertices, record them on a stack, and reinsert them in reverse order with a greedy color choice. With adjacency lists and maintained degrees, elimination and reconstruction can be implemented in $O(|V|+|E|)$ time when a suitable degeneracy bound is supplied.

The contrapositive says that if $\chi(G)\ge k+2$, then some nonempty induced subgraph has minimum degree at least $k+1$. Thus chromatic difficulty forces a locally dense core, though a complete-minor conclusion requires more work.

\section{The Wagner bridge}

\begin{definition}[Planarity by forbidden minors]
A finite graph $G$ is planar when it contains neither $K_5$ nor $K_{3,3}$ as a minor.
\end{definition}

This is the forbidden-minor characterization of geometric planarity.

\begin{statement}[Four Color Theorem]
Every finite planar graph is four-colorable.
\end{statement}

\begin{statement}[$K_5$ Hadwiger coloring statement]
Every finite graph that is not four-colorable contains $K_5$ as a minor.
\end{statement}

This is indexed case $k=4$ because its target is $K_{4+1}$.

\begin{statement}[Wagner minor-free coloring statement]
Every finite graph with no $K_5$ minor is four-colorable.
\end{statement}

\begin{theorem}[Wagner contrapositive equivalence]
The $K_5$ Hadwiger coloring statement and the Wagner minor-free coloring statement are logically equivalent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume the first statement. A graph with no $K_5$ minor cannot fail to be four-colorable, since failure would produce that forbidden minor. Conversely, assume the minor-free statement. A non-four-colorable graph cannot be $K_5$-minor-free, because then the assumption would provide a four-coloring. Thus it has a $K_5$ minor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Wagner forward implication]
The $K_5$ Hadwiger coloring statement implies the Four Color Theorem.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be planar. It has no $K_5$ minor. By the contrapositive equivalence it is four-colorable. Equivalently, if it were not four-colorable, the assumed $K_5$ statement would contradict planarity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The indexed Hadwiger coloring case $k=4$ implies the Four Color Theorem.
\end{corollary}

Only this forward logical bridge is established. The reverse connection requires a structural description of $K_5$-minor-free graphs in terms of planar graphs and the Wagner graph, assembled through clique-sums. That decomposition does not follow from contraposition alone.

The $K_{3,3}$ exclusion is needed to characterize planarity, but the forward proof uses only that every planar graph is $K_5$-minor-free. Therefore four-coloring all $K_5$-minor-free graphs is stronger than four-coloring planar graphs.

\section{Average degree and density}\label{sec:density}

For a finite graph define
\[
\overline d(G)=
\begin{cases}
0,& |V(G)|=0,\\[3pt]
\displaystyle\frac{1}{|V(G)|}\sum_{v\in V(G)}\deg_G(v),& |V(G)|>0.
\end{cases}
\]
The handshake identity gives $\overline d(G)=2|E(G)|/|V(G)|$ for nonempty $G$.

\begin{theorem}[Kostochka--Thomason density theorem, stated]
There exists a universal constant $c>0$ such that, for every positive integer $t$ and every finite graph $G$,
\[
\overline d(G)\ge c\,t\sqrt{\log t}
\]
implies that $G$ contains $K_t$ as a minor.
\end{theorem}

No proof is supplied here. The theorem records the asymptotically correct scale at which average degree forces complete minors. It shows that connected branch sets can route an all-to-all pattern even when no comparably large clique is visible.

Degeneracy is a complementary elementary lens. Sparse induced subgraphs permit greedy coloring; conversely, high chromatic number creates an induced subgraph of high minimum degree. The density theorem indicates how density may force complete minors, although matching the exact chromatic threshold remains delicate.

\section{Algorithms and finite experiments}

\subsection{Exact coloring}
An exact algorithm tests $q=0,1,\ldots,n$ and backtracks through color assignments. It chooses an uncolored vertex and assigns a color absent from its colored neighborhood. The first feasible $q$ is $\chi(G)$. For fixed $q$, the worst-case search considers $q^n$ assignments, though saturation and degree orderings help in small instances.

\subsection{Checking a minor certificate}
Given $B_1,\ldots,B_t$, a verifier checks nonemptiness, disjointness, connectivity by breadth-first search within each set, and an edge between every pair of branch sets. This is polynomial in the host size and certificate size. Thus finding a model may be difficult, but checking a proposed model is efficient.

\subsection{Exhaustive branch-set search}
To seek a $K_t$ model, label every host vertex by $0,1,\ldots,t$, where $0$ means unused and label $i$ places the vertex in $B_i$. There are at most $(t+1)^n$ assignments. Reject any assignment with an empty or disconnected branch set, overlap, or a missing pairwise contact. Ordering branch sets by their least vertices breaks permutation symmetry.

There are
\[
2^{\binom n2}
\]
labeled simple graphs on $n$ vertices. Exhaustively computing $\chi(G)$ and searching for $K_{\chi(G)}$ therefore gives a small-order testing laboratory, not a scalable proof method.

\subsection{Examples}
For an empty nonempty graph, $\chi(G)=1$ and any singleton witnesses $K_1$. A graph consisting of one edge and isolated vertices has chromatic number $2$, and the endpoints witness $K_2$. For $K_t$, singleton branch sets witness both chromatic number and complete-minor order $t$. An odd cycle has chromatic number $3$; three connected arcs can form a triangle model. A tree with an edge contains $K_2$ but no $K_3$ minor, because deletion and contraction preserve acyclicity.

\section{Applications and scope}

Coloring represents conflicts in scheduling, frequency assignment, register allocation, and labeling. A clique is an immediate obstruction: all its vertices compete pairwise. A complete minor is distributed: each branch set can be a connected subsystem, while pairwise contacts among subsystems encode robust interaction after internal detail is compressed.

The Hadwiger number consequently measures the largest all-to-all interaction pattern recoverable under network coarse-graining. It can reveal complexity routed through paths and regions that the clique number misses. Degeneracy orderings have separate algorithmic value in sparse graph coloring, triangle enumeration, and sparse matrix methods.

The Wagner bridge places map coloring within a broader theory. Proving the $K_5$ statement for all finite graphs is enough to four-color planar graphs. The brevity of the implication reflects the strength concentrated in its premise.

The scope should be explicit. Reflexivity, spanning-subgraph inclusion, host monotonicity, clique models, the first two indexed cases, degeneracy coloring, and the forward Wagner bridge have been proved. Full minor transitivity and the indexed cases from $k=2$ onward are not derived. The logical contrapositive equivalence does not replace Wagner's structural decomposition. The density theorem was stated for context, not proved.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Branch-set transitivity.} Compose a model of $H$ in $G$ with a model of $K$ in $H$ by taking unions of first-level branch sets. Prove nonemptiness, disjointness, connectivity, and target adjacency.
\item \textbf{The triangle case.} Establish the odd-cycle characterization of non-bipartite graphs and contract an odd cycle to three connected branch sets.
\item \textbf{The four-clique case.} Prove that every graph with no proper three-coloring contains a $K_4$ minor through the classical low-order theorem rather than finite enumeration.
\item \textbf{Wagner's structural bridge.} Decompose finite $K_5$-minor-free graphs by clique-sums into planar pieces and the Wagner graph, then derive the reverse implication from the Four Color Theorem.
\item \textbf{Known cases through six colors.} Establish all indexed cases $k\le5$. The endpoint states that every non-five-colorable finite graph contains a $K_6$ minor and should identify explicitly where the Four Color Theorem enters.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The branch-set model turns minors into geometric certificates: disjoint connected territories linked according to a target graph. Every graph is its own minor, spanning subgraphs are minors, adding host edges preserves models, and every clique supplies a complete minor. These facts establish the first two indexed coloring cases and the inequality $\omega(G)\le h(G)$.

Degeneracy supplies a constructive bridge from local sparsity to global colorability. At order five, contraposition equates ``non-four-colorable implies a $K_5$ minor'' with ``$K_5$-minor-free implies four-colorable,'' and planarity then yields the Four Color Theorem.

Hadwiger's conjecture predicts a universal conversion between two forms of complexity. Chromatic number counts the incompatible labels a graph demands. The Hadwiger number measures the complete interaction pattern surviving after connected regions are compressed. The conjecture says that the latter always dominates the former.

\end{document}

